      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combine a major advance in measurement with broad consequences for fundamental physics, timekeeping, and tests of relativity.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently pioneered complementary clock approaches; both contributions are central to the field's development.",
        "maturity": "The technique has been repeatedly demonstrated and continues to improve, with a substantial record of precision measurements.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundary between the original clock concepts and later technical advances would need careful historical review."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michel Della Negra", "Peter Jenni", "Tejinder S. Virdee"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Higgs boson discovery completed a central prediction of particle physics and stands among the most consequential experimental discoveries of the past half century.",
        "attribution": "These leaders represent the two collaborations that made the discovery possible; credit for a result of this scale is necessarily distributed across thousands of contributors.",
        "maturity": "The discovery is exceptionally well confirmed by multiple decay channels and subsequent measurements.",
        "uncertainties": ["Selecting a few individuals to represent the ATLAS and CMS collaborations is intrinsically difficult and risks omitting essential contributions."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alfred Y. Cho", "Federico Capasso", "Jérôme Faist"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser was a distinctive invention that opened new spectral regions to compact, tunable laser sources and enabled important spectroscopy and sensing.",
        "attribution": "Cho's semiconductor heterostructure advances and the later device conception and demonstration by Capasso and Faist form a clear, though staged, history of contribution.",
        "maturity": "The laser has been developed, reproduced, and applied over decades across a wide range of operating conditions.",
        "uncertainties": ["The precise division of credit between enabling heterostructure work and the specific invention should be checked against the original record."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Francis Halzen"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Detecting high-energy astrophysical neutrinos opened a new observational window on the universe and established neutrino astronomy as a field.",
        "attribution": "Halzen's leadership and foundational role in IceCube are substantial, while the discovery itself depended on a large collaboration and many instrumental contributions.",
        "maturity": "The signal has been established over years of observation, with growing evidence connecting neutrinos to astrophysical sources.",
        "uncertainties": ["The first-detection claim and the individual credit assigned to a collaboration-scale discovery need careful scrutiny."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B039",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Bennett", "Lyman A. Page Jr."],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precise full-sky mapping of the cosmic microwave background transformed cosmology into a precision observational science.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Page are closely associated with the mission's scientific and instrumental work; the contribution of the broader team is also indispensable.",
        "maturity": "The measurements have been extensively checked and incorporated into a stable cosmological framework.",
        "uncertainties": ["The distinction between WMAP's advance and the earlier COBE measurements, already recognized by the Nobel Prize, requires careful definition."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B015",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Eli Yablonovitch", "John D. Joannopoulos", "Sajeev John"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The concept and realization of photonic crystals established a powerful way to control light through engineered periodic structures, with broad influence in optics.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated key ideas; Joannopoulos helped develop the theoretical framework and its reach.",
        "maturity": "The underlying physics is well established and has been realized in many materials and devices.",
        "uncertainties": ["The independent origins and division between conceptual, theoretical, and experimental contributions should be resolved precisely."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexandre Blais", "Andreas Wallraff", "Robert J. Schoelkopf"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit quantum electrodynamics made coherent light–matter interaction accessible in engineered solid-state devices and became a foundation for superconducting quantum technology.",
        "attribution": "Blais supplied influential theory, while Wallraff and Schoelkopf led landmark experimental demonstrations and development.",
        "maturity": "The central effects have been reproduced widely and underpin a mature research area, though the broader technology continues to evolve.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field has many essential contributors, and priority across the theoretical proposal and first demonstrations must be weighed carefully."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B048",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Peter W. Shor", "Andrew M. Steane", "Alexei Kitaev"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed in principle how fragile quantum information could be protected, changing the prospects for quantum computation and shaping modern quantum information physics.",
        "attribution": "Shor, Steane, and Kitaev made distinct foundational contributions to codes and fault-tolerant ideas.",
        "maturity": "The theory is established and experimental demonstrations have advanced substantially, although scalable fault-tolerant operation remains an ongoing challenge.",
        "uncertainties": ["It is not yet clear which achievements mark a sufficiently complete experimental realization of the original promise."]
      }
    }
  ]
